## By the Numbers: The Year of the Recall

781 food recalls this year and counting--a significant spike compared to previous years. Notably, Class I recalls, which pose the highest risk to consumer health, make up 217 of these cases. This trend raises questions about processes within food manufacturing and oversight.

## Repeat Offenders: A Risky Trend

95 companies have found themselves on the repeat offender list, with brands like Spring & Mulberry Inc. leading the infamous ranking with 11 recalls, all Class I. Such consistency in failing safety standards is troubling and warrants consumer vigilance.

## Recent Incidents

Just this month, Adams Extract & Spice LLC issued multiple recalls due to undeclared sesame allergens in their spice products--a stark reminder that label accuracy can mean the difference between safety and hospitalization for many consumers.

## The Consumer's Role

While regulatory bodies continue to enforce standards, the transparency and accessibility of recall data empower consumers to make safer choices. Reviewing recall lists and understanding brand histories are steps toward safeguarding your pantry.
